WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:04.310
- One thing that I'm really
starting to notice is that

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:07.810
it's becoming extremely
difficult not to spend all

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:10.700
of our time on social
media, on the internet

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:13.540
and all of that during
these times of isolation,

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:15.810
as if it wasn't already a huge problem.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:17.610
And it kind of makes sense
when you think about it.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:20.670
If people are locked inside
their houses all day long

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:23.090
then obviously they're
gonna start gravitating

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:26.870
towards these pleasure boxes,
these entertainment cubes.

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:28.410
And I don't wanna be another video

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:31.340
that's just like
smartphone bad, books good

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:33.780
but I do think that the growing influence

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:37.740
of these algorithm heavy
websites needs to be talked about

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:40.950
because the majority of
these websites or these apps

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:42.880
are designed from the ground up

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.250
to be as addictive as possible.

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:47.077
I just finished watching the documentary,

00:00:47.077 --> 00:00:50.250
"The Social Dilemma" on Netflix,
and it really reminded me

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.050
of some of the things that I also read

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.360
in "Digital Minimalism" by Cal Newport.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.010
And that is that for
these big tech companies

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:59.370
our attention is their currency.

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:01.440
We're not the customer, we're the product.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.960
The customer of Facebook and Instagram

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.320
and whatever are the third-party companies

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:08.500
that have advertising on their platform.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.360
And that gets advertised to us.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:13.210
So it's in big tech's best interest

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:16.370
to keep us hooked on these
apps for as long as possible.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.750
And that's why they're
so algorithm dependent

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:21.510
because there hasn't
been a tool in history

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:24.480
that has been so powerful at predicting

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:25.890
what you're going to do next.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.350
What your likes and
what your dislikes are,

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.610
what videos you're most likely to binge,

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:32.580
what topics you're most interested in,

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.070
these websites are unbelievably good

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:38.570
at just serving you a video
that you're likely to watch

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:40.910
and that you're likely to
waste a lot of time on.

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:43.980
Maybe this video, this type
of self-improvement content

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:45.550
is something that you're likely to binge

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:48.180
which is why I ended up
on your YouTube homepage.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:50.090
These algorithms are powerful.

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:52.720
And I don't wanna be all
doom and gloom about this.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:55.070
I'm not saying that there's
nothing we can do about it,

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:58.290
but I think it is important to
be conscious of our behavior

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:00.290
because these apps rely so heavily

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:02.050
on our unconscious behavior.

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:03.300
So what's the solution?

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:06.470
It would probably be pretty
lonely if we just cut ourselves

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:09.970
off from technology altogether,
and then also just lived

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:12.170
in an empty house by ourselves for months

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:14.370
and months and months until
this whole thing blows over.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:16.500
But at the same time we
do have to be proactive

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.070
with finding solutions I think.

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:21.370
Otherwise our internet use is
gonna get so out of control

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:24.050
and occupy like 90% of our day

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:26.500
and when the pandemic is actually over,

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:29.280
we're still gonna have this
habit of just waiting up

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.010
and going on the internet
for like 90% of the day

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:32.843
and then going to bed.

00:02:32.843 --> 00:02:35.830
And I'm not sure if I have
the answer to this problem

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:37.810
because everybody's life is so different.

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:40.260
So I figured it'd be really
cool to talk to my friend

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:44.010
and fellow creator, Nathaniel
Drew about this issue

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:47.010
and try to get his thoughts
on maybe some solutions.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.270
And hopefully through our conversation

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:51.480
you guys can start
thinking about a game plan

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.810
that makes sense for you and your life.

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:56.680
So Nathaniel, what do we
do about this problem?

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:58.263
- There is a lot we can do about it.

00:02:58.263 --> 00:03:00.490
This is nowhere near a sort of situation

00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:02.300
where it's like out of our control,

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:04.300
that's it, we live in a dystopia.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:07.010
I'm seeing more and more
people wake up to this

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:10.730
and I find that very encouraging
and I have felt in myself

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:13.390
over the last year, particularly
because of the imbalances

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:15.080
that I feel in my life and in the world

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:19.130
that I am also making an
effort to not feel like

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:22.120
I'm at the mercy of this machine learning

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:23.630
that is deciding everything for me.

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:26.800
I do believe I have control
over this to a certain extent.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:29.580
And if that means that at times I need

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:32.870
to remove myself from it or
change things up then, so be it.

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:34.520
But I have control over that.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:35.353
- Right off the bat.

00:03:35.353 --> 00:03:37.300
I love this point that Nathan makes,

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.570
my entire last video was about how having

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:43.220
an internal locus of control
is extremely important

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:46.540
when everything around us seems
to be so out of our control.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:48.290
We can't control the pandemic.

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:50.510
We can't control the economy.

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:53.080
We can't control a lot in our lives

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:56.480
but we do have control over
our actions and our behaviors.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:59.300
And I feel like social
media's influence on us

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:00.970
is a great example of something

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:03.430
that we have the power
to be proactive about.

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:06.116
- I think this is really
interesting, internal locus.

00:04:06.116 --> 00:04:07.680
(beep) Yeah
- Yeah dude.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:08.513
- If you're trying to break

00:04:08.513 --> 00:04:11.280
out of an algorithmically controlled life,

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:14.130
I would say there's two things
to take into consideration.

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:16.320
One, there's a level of
personal accountability.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.610
Take a level of ownership
over your decisions

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:19.790
and how you're spending your time.

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:22.760
Like there's no alternative
to that, you can.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:25.290
There's no workaround,
but there's also a level

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:27.220
of understanding that helps me feel

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:30.580
a little bit more self-compassionate
in when I recognize

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:33.720
that, okay, I am
susceptible to distractions

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:37.890
and brightly colored
interesting looking thumbnails,

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:39.170
let's say.

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:42.120
And so, I take steps to
sort of remove it from

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:43.090
or add friction, right?

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:46.140
Like make it all a little
bit less accessible.

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:48.200
And I think it's been a process, dude.

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:49.090
I say this all the time.

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:50.470
I feel like I sound like a broken record

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:53.610
but I'm always trying things
out to see what works.

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:56.120
And it's been a multi-month,
multi-year process

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:58.130
of me trying to figure
out what is the life

00:04:58.130 --> 00:05:01.285
that I want to live and what is the level

00:05:01.285 --> 00:05:04.480
of information consumption
that is right for me.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:05.810
I find I have to just check in

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:08.910
with myself regularly, constantly, almost.

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:11.150
- So if how we spend
our time is our choice

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:13.130
and we know that we probably
shouldn't be spending

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:15.140
as much time on social media,

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:17.280
I think it's really
important that we take out

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:20.170
a good old-fashioned pen
and paper and try to think

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:23.190
about how we would ideally
like to spend our day.

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:25.590
How much time do you wanna
be spending on social media?

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:27.220
And you know, maybe that's not zero.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:30.290
Maybe you do wanna engage
in these like pleasures

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:32.230
a little bit, but be intentional with it.

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:34.530
Try to plan out how much
time you actually want

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:37.680
to be spending and be
conscious of that decision.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:41.150
Otherwise, YouTube and Reddit
and Facebook are really good

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:43.120
at just stealing your time from you

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:44.430
if you don't have a plan.

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:47.010
And once you've made this plan,
once you've kind of designed

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:49.510
the structure around your social media use

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:50.810
I think there's a lot of tools.

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:53.470
I think Apple has one
if you have an iPhone,

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:55.170
Android has one if you have an Android

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:58.200
where you can actually
set timers certain apps.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.840
I'd highly recommend you
look up a YouTube video on

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:04.660
how to set timers on apps
depending on what OS you have,

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:06.310
that'll add an element of friction

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:09.230
and help you stay on your goal.

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:12.080
Yeah, I think that's a really
like level headed approach

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:14.360
because I feel like so many
people they get so fed up

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:17.720
with all the control that social
media has over their lives

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:20.880
and they get very extreme about it.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:23.110
So they'll like smash
their phone with a hammer

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:26.540
or like install like
blockers on their computer.

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:28.160
And they're like, Oh,
I'm deleting my Facebook.

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:31.010
I'm never going on any
of these apps again.

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:34.040
I'm retaking control of my
life, but I feel like 99%

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:36.720
of people who do that end up
kind of like bouncing back.

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:38.900
They underestimate the gravity of like

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:41.840
what this social media is
replacing in your life.

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:44.810
If you find yourself constantly
escaping from reality

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:48.020
then you need to work really
hard to create a reality

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:49.420
you don't want to from.

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:51.180
- It's easy to forget about this.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:54.870
Being too externally focused,
thinking there's solutions

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:57.750
in terms of systems or
how I should live my life

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.660
or what I should be doing with my time

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:01.940
that will solve everything.

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:04.140
And that is the part of the equation

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:05.250
but it's not the whole thing.

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:08.700
To get to a place where
you're living a life

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:11.240
that you don't necessarily
wanna escape from,

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:12.300
there's an internal element,

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:13.150
I don't know if that makes sense.

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:15.480
It's sort of like, what is your
relationship with yourself.

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:18.410
- Yeah, I think a lot of
us use technology as a form

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:21.270
of escapism, which I can
totally relate to, by the way.

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:24.480
I know personally that,
especially in times like this

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:27.250
when I tried to just not spend all day

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:30.320
on Facebook or Instagram or even YouTube

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:35.230
sometimes an anxiety or discomfort
starts to stir within me

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:36.800
and it's very uncomfortable.

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:39.640
It actually feels almost
physically painful

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:42.860
to remove yourself from technology

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:45.150
and just have a more boring day

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:47.380
or at least a less stimulating day.

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:49.150
But I think it's extremely important

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:51.690
that we engage with
this side of ourselves.

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:54.990
I think it's extremely important
to really feel this boredom

00:07:54.990 --> 00:07:58.350
and to feel this anxiety so
that we can actually work

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:01.030
through it and stop escaping
from it all the time.

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:02.860
And there are a couple
of things that you can do

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:05.680
to sort of properly work
through these emotions

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:10.340
and become a lot more
okay with being alone,

00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:13.480
being cut off from this
constant flow of information.

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:16.180
One of my favorite ways of
doing that is mindfulness.

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:19.020
Learning how to just observe
your negative thoughts

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:21.230
from sort of a third person perspective

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:23.470
and also journaling, writing down things

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:26.440
that you're concerned about
and writing down a game plan

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:28.810
for how you might
overcome those obstacles.

00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:30.160
And honestly, just get outside.

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:31.790
Get out of your house, go in nature.

00:08:31.790 --> 00:08:34.000
There are safe ways to leave your house.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:36.380
And I honestly think
that one of the best ways

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:38.960
to clear your head is to sort of reconnect

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:40.750
with that primal aspect of you.

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:43.890
Go outside, go for a walk
in nature, go for a hike,

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:45.990
exercise as much as possible.

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:48.470
There are ways that
you can spend time away

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:51.320
from social media apps that
are actually kind of fun

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:53.410
and will make you feel better.

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:56.800
- That's why I'm saying that
this period of a lockdown

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:00.400
and time alone, it was
painful and it was difficult

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:03.810
especially early last year,
but I'm really, really grateful

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:06.520
that I went through this because
it has completely shifted

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:09.580
how I spend time with myself.

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:12.670
And I think I kind of saw more clearly

00:09:12.670 --> 00:09:15.500
than I've ever seen before
that it all starts from there.

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:17.440
So if I can't solve that

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:20.610
no matter what I try to do
any self-improvement sense

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:22.690
I will never get to that sort of lifestyle

00:09:22.690 --> 00:09:25.040
that makes me feel like
I don't need to escape.

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:27.570
- Ultimately though, even
the fact that you're watching

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:30.160
this video hopefully
has caused you to think

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:32.790
about the role that you want social media

00:09:32.790 --> 00:09:35.380
and the internet in general
to play in your lives.

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:37.310
And also be more cognizant of the fact

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:40.700
that a lot of these websites
that you like to spend time on

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:43.260
are profiting greatly from your attention.

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:46.540
They want to keep you hooked
into them as much as possible

00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:48.780
even at the expense of your mental health

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:50.530
and your overall sense of wellbeing.

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:53.420
And hopefully all this will
help you spend your time

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:55.470
in a way that you want to be spending it

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:58.723
not how Lord Zucc wants you to spend it.

